course: dictionary
lesson: 15108
title: Sabotage: spoiling something on purpose
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Sabotage means damaging or spoiling something on purpose, usually to stop an enemy or a rival. Look for the clue that someone meant to do harm. «The resistance fighters sabotaged the railway so the enemy trains could not run.» The fighters did not break the railway by accident. They broke it on purpose to stop the enemy.
explain: Sabotage is often secret. «Ben secretly hid his sister's costume the night before the school play.» Hiding the costume is a plan to spoil her chance, so this is sabotage. Ask yourself: did someone wreck it or spoil it on purpose? If yes, sabotage is the right word.
explain: You can also sabotage yourself. «Tom skipped every practice, then said he would never win.» Tom is spoiling his own chance, which is called self-sabotage. Use your context clues to decide whether the spoiling is done to an enemy, a rival, a team, or to yourself.
fact: Sabotage means damaging or destroying something on purpose, especially machines, roads or plans.
fact: «Sabotage» can be a noun («an act of sabotage») or a verb («they sabotaged the machine»).
fact: In World War II, resistance fighters sabotaged railways and factories to slow down the enemy.
fact: «Self-sabotage» means spoiling your own chance or plan, often without meaning to.
fact: A good check is to swap in the word «spoil on purpose» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The resistance fighters sabotaged the railway so the enemy trains could not run.» What does sabotaged mean here? | Painted bright colours | *Damaged on purpose to stop something working | Cleaned very carefully | Built a brand new bridge
exercise: «Ben secretly hid his sister's costume the night before the school play.» What is this an example of? | A surprise party | A kind favour | *Sabotage, spoiling someone's chance on purpose | A tidy-up
exercise: «They sabotaged the machine yesterday.» Find and copy the verb form of sabotage. | yesterday | *sabotaged | machine | They
test: «Lily's friend sabotaged her science project by hiding the glue.» Does sabotage mean helping or spoiling? | Finishing early | Forgetting | Helping | *Spoiling on purpose
test: Tom skipped every practice, then said he would never win. What is this called? | Good luck | *Self-sabotage | Teamwork | Practice makes perfect
exam: «The villain tried to sabotage the spaceship's engine, but the hero stopped him.» What does sabotage mean? | To fly the spaceship faster | *To damage something on purpose | To make a new friend | To polish the engine
